Notes on LDA: 
-----------------------------------------------------
linear discriminat analysis: finda linear combinations of neurons that best separate classes (stimulus)
doesn't need lots of parameters so less likely to overfit

say we have 100 neurons and 3 conditions, lda finds the best way to project 100 dimensions down to 2 dimensions
(num_classes - 1) that maximizes separation between conditions. It does this by maximizing between-class variance / within-class variance

between-class: how far aparat are the condition means
within-class: how much do trials of same condition vary

if monet trials all cluster tight but far form natural trials, it is easy to separate
for new data points it sees which side of boundaries it falls on

for this week we are only looking at neuron 4 from roiresponse3 because idk i like four yay

-----------------------------------------------------
LDA Class:
-----------------------------------------------------

train_test_split(X, y, test_size = 0.2, stratify - y)
  x = our data (trials x neurons), y = labels (0, 1, 2, for conditions)
  test_size = 0.2 means 20% for testing
  stratify = y means keep same proportion of each condition in train and test
  returns X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test

LinearDiscriminantAnalysis()
  creates the model object
  lda.fit(X_train, y_train) = trains it on training data
  lda.predict(X_test) = predicts labels for test data
  lda.coef_ = the weights showing which neurons are important

classification_report(y_test, y_pred)
  shows precision, recall, f1 score for each condition
  precision = of the trials we predicted as monet, how many actually were monet
  recall = of all the actual monet trials, how any did we catch
  f1 = harmonic mean of precision and recall

