Decoding Thoughts:
-----------------------------------------------------
In future weeks we could try logistic regresison with L2 regularization, random forest to see if no nlinear interactions help
Neural nets dont have enough data so probly not this. 

How to decided which is best: test accuracy on held out data, look at confusion matrices to see what conditions get mixed up
check if it generalizes, computational cost too ig

Why linear might be enough: we are already averaging over time, stimulus categores are pretty different, 
non linear might just fit noise

Averaging acitivity acorss entire stimulus window: each clip is like 5-10sec we take all the neural activity during that
window, average it to get one number per neuron per trial. Reduces nose by averaging, gives us one clear feature vector per trial,
cons that it throws away temporal information, might miss important acivity like oscillating or smth, if conditions differ in timing not magnitude 
we will miss it.

Looking at specific time points: pick specific moments like stimulus onset, peack response, offset, or take
activity at every timepoint separately, each trial becomes multiple feature vectors. Pros: keeps temporal information
can see if neurons respond early or late. Cons: way more features, need more data to avoid overfitting. 

Trial averaging: say monet1 is shown 5 times, average the neural responses across those 5 presentations, now we have 1 averaged trial
for monet1 instead of 5 noisy trials. Pros: reduce trial to trial like variability the difference in it, less noise in data. Cons:
way fewer training samples, assumes all presentations are identical.

Time moment decoding: dont average over time, train separate decoder for each timepoint, at 100ms after stimulus, can we decode what mice see,
at 500ms after stimulus can we decode what they see etc. Pros: see when information emerges in the population, can track how representation
changes over time, can understand the dynamic like oscillation or ramp up of a neuron. Cons: need to train like many decoders.

Thoughts: start with averaging, try time moment decoding, try different time windows (after first couple of seconds or peak response times), 
propbably not trial by averaging since we dont have many repeats and losing samples. 

What we are questioning: does the information we care about live in the magnitude of response (avearaged activity) or the
pattern over time (dynamics) for distinguishing big catecories like from naturla vs monet the magnitude is probably enough but for
distinguishing specific clips might need dynamic approach. 

What decoding depends on: if stimuli are really different lengths averaging could be hard since you are comparing a 3 second clip to
10 second clip, current approach uses fixed windows size to help, but maybe initial repsonse is more important and the rest is just sustained activity

What to check in the future: how variable is stimulus duration, do responses adapt over time (get weaker). Sliding time frame (decode from 0 -2, 1-3, 2-4), 
early vs late (first half vs second half of stimulus), peak response (just the max)

