Hyperparameters:
-----------------------------------------------------
hyperparameters: settings you make before training that affect how the model learns not learned from data

  Ex: how many neurons to use, what time window to use, train/test split ratio

For lda specifically: 

Solver: svd, lsqr, eigen, if you have more feautres than samples use lsqr or eigen but we have way more samples
than features so svd is being used. 

Shrinkage: regularizaiton to prevent overfitting, options: none, auto, num between 0 and 1. Shrinks covariance matrix 
toward diagnol to make it more stable. When to use: if you have high dimensional data or class covariances. It might help
since if using tons of neurons try auto if overfitting

Num of Nuerons: how many neurons to include in analysis, rn we use 100. more neurons = more info but also more noise and slower
computation, can try 10, 25, 100, 200 to see what gives best test accuracy

Time Window Size: how much neural activity to anayze per trial, rn we use 5 seconds. Longer means more data but might include irrelevant activty
shorter = less noise but might miss slow responses, can try 2s 5s 10s or full stimulus duration


